-| UI | `pi-minimalist` (npm, pinned to 0.3.1: one-line tool rows, consecutive calls grouped, every turn's thinking and text kept in order; the `minimalist` block keeps streaming thinking open and leaves `edit`/`write` to pi's own boxes so changes are never folded away) plus `split-diff.ts`, which draws edit diffs old | new inside pi's edit box at 120+ columns using `renderSplitDiff` from `pi-tidy` (npm, pinned to 0.4.12, installed with `"extensions": []` so none of its own patches load; it reads pi's internal `preview` field and the box's child layout, guarded by `tests/split-diff.test.ts`), plus four copied files with credit headers: `polish-chrome.ts` (pi-polish 0.1.3 `chrome.ts`: ❯/● markers in column 1 with text in column 3, the gutter pi's tool rows and notices already use, which pi's own `outputPad` (0 or 1) can't give; spinner; the waiting phase keyed on `ask_user`; upstream's hidden-thinking blanking cut. 0.1.5 adds only features, no fixes), `context-bar.ts` (pi-context-bar, patched: bar fills against compact-cap's live threshold read through `lib/compact-cap-state.ts`, cwd name before the branch, heads-up cost in the `$` total, compact `▰▱ 13k/150k 9%` meter, cwd and branch flush left with cache, context, model and cost flush right; in a half split it wraps to two lines with the cost pinned top right after the model), `heads-up.ts` (pi-heads-up, patched: one note per 3+-tool task as a chat entry, thinking off, "nothing to flag" recorded but not drawn), `background.ts` (pi-background, patched; see Jobs). Clicked file links open in nvim through `config/kitty/open-actions.conf`; pi's fullscreen TUI captures the mouse and would run macOS `open` (VLC for `.ts`), so `kitty-links.ts` hands `file://` clicks to `kitty @ action open_url`. | Copies, not packages, because each needed a patch; inherited `any` stays, new lines add none. Heads-up costs ~$0.03 per note at a ~90% cache hit and its cost is shown, not hidden. Re-copy upstream fixes by hand. |
-| Jobs | `background.ts`: `bg_run`/`bg_status`/`bg_kill`, bash capped at 120 s default / 600 s max (scout and `timeout …` exempt), and a zero-token watchdog (`lib/watchdog.ts`, pure, tested). Foreground bash is pi's own tool with wrapped operations: no output, CPU or network for 90 s kills the process group (`killed: idle 90s …`); `ssh`/`docker`/`kubectl`/`scout`/`timeout` are exempt. `bg_run` jobs are never killed; a quiet idle job wakes pi at 5/15/45 min, a busy silent one at 30 min, at most 3 wakes. Declared signals win: `$PI_HEARTBEAT` (touch it) and `bg_run health` (checked every 30 s). Job messages are steered into a running turn (seen before its next model call) and start a turn only when pi is idle. Bash has four owners, one job each: `background.ts` caps the timeout (`tool_call`) and runs the command (re-registers pi's own bash definition with watched operations, pi's documented override pattern, so schema and renderer are unchanged); `pi-rtk-optimizer` rewrites the command text in its own `tool_call`, which runs after ours because local extensions load before packages, and the watchdog's exemptions still match the rewritten `rtk docker …` (`tests/watchdog.test.ts`); `worker-sensors.ts` blocks commands in workers only; `pi-minimalist` draws the rows. Job tails (completion message, watchdog wake, `bg_status`) collapse `\r` progress redraws to the text a terminal would show, so a progress bar is one line, not thousands of chars (2026-10-28). Jobs do not outlive pi: `session_shutdown` aborts every running job's controller and clears the job table, so nothing started with `bg_run` survives the session that started it. | Every long-running path needs a limit or a health signal. Evidence first: exit, wake_on, declared signal, output, then CPU/network/process change; a failed `ps`/`nettop` never kills or wakes. `nettop` must run with `-n` (5.1 s per call without it). Spiked on 1.4.0: a 0.05 s CPU threshold killed a throttled download, so any increase counts. |
-| Stream stalls | `extensions/stream-watchdog.ts` (main session and scout children): no provider stream event for 5 min (10 min at xhigh/max or in a worker) aborts the response and sends one "continue" message; a second stall in a row stops loudly. `bin/scout` adds an outer guard: a stale child session file with no tool running is killed after 600/900 s, verdict `CANNOT-RUN model stream stalled`. | pi's `httpIdleTimeoutMs` never reaches the Bedrock path (`bedrock-converse-stream.js` passes only the abort signal); work sessions hung 8–47 min until killed by hand. Spiked: `agent_before_settle` does not fire after an abort, so the resume goes through `agent_settled`; a fake Bedrock hang now ends in seconds instead of never. Local providers (localhost) are exempt. |
-| Bedrock thinking | `extensions/bedrock-reasoning.ts` adds `reasoning.summary: "auto"` to Bedrock GPT requests (main session and scouts). Use `us.`/`global.openai.gpt-5.6-luna`; the bare id fails. It also drops `reasoningContent.reasoningText` from replayed GPT assistant messages; encrypted reasoning (`redactedContent`) stays. | pi sends only `reasoning.effort`, so Converse returned no thinking text (20-char placeholder vs 373 chars on the work laptop). pi-ai replays non-Claude thinking as `reasoningText`, which Bedrock GPT rejects, so every Sol request after a tool call failed (work, 10-27). Replaces the hand-written work "mantle" extension, which defaulted to a region without the endpoint (404). |
-| Tracking | Files in `~/Dev/dotfiles/config/pi/`, symlinked into `~/.pi/agent/`. Pi itself is the managed install (`~/.pi/agent/install/releases/<version>`, `current-version` picks one, `~/bin/pi` → `~/.pi/agent/bin/pi` launcher; `pi update` swaps releases), NOT brew or npm-global — the brew formula lags releases (was pinned 0.80.6 when npm had 0.80.10) and nvm-global moved with the node version. `tests/pi-package.ts` follows `current-version` first, the `pi` on PATH otherwise. | Mirrors the OpenCode dotfiles pattern; version-controlled. |
-| Placement | Put each job in the tool that owns it (git hook, CLI, script): one job per module, few links between modules, the simplest form that works. A harness extension needs a reason: the job lives inside the harness (stream, compaction, rendering, in-loop retry) or no tool can see it. No pi extension for git or file work a hook or script can do. | Deterministic, harness-agnostic, low coupling: the same behavior under pi, Claude Code and a bare shell. The rule is in `agents-md/standards.md`, so every harness built from the fragments carries it. |
-| Scout git guard | `bin/guard/git` is first on PATH in every scout child, read and `--rw`. It blocks push, pull, merge, rebase, reset, clean, stash, checkout, switch and restore (plus commit and add for read scouts), only against the scout's own repo; exit 1 is a refused command, exit 77 means the guard is misconfigured (no real git) and nothing can run. | A token scan misses wrapper spellings; PATH catches every call of `git` by name. A tripwire, not a sandbox: an absolute `/usr/bin/git` or `git -c alias.x=...` gets past. |
-| Auto-run git hooks | `config/git/bin/auto-guard` runs from `init.templateDir` stubs (new repos, `home/.gitconfig`), from one line `git-hooks-install` inserts into existing sh/bash hooks, from husky's `~/.config/husky/init.sh`, and from dotfiles' `githooks/`. While a root `AUTO.md` exists it refuses commits and merge commits on the default branch and every push; otherwise it is silent. | Git enforces it under any harness. `--no-verify`, fast-forward merges and `update-ref` pass, so it is a tripwire. |
-| Worktrees | Branches come first; worktrees only when two trees must be live (the git-patterns skill says so). worktrunk (`wt`) copies every gitignored file (reflinked) with global excludes and no include list, since `.worktreeinclude` would put a new dotfile in every repo. `config/git/bin/worktree-setup` runs from post-checkout and wt's post-start (first wins) and installs only when a lockfile differs from main's. Main is primary for env files and `worktree-setup sync` pushes them (main wins); no symlinks. | One copy path for npm and pnpm repos, near-zero disk on APFS, and agents never edit env files. |
+| Agent shape | One agent; one opt-in read-only scout as a child `pi` behind `bin/scout`. | `pi-subagents` cost 8,277 resident tokens per turn for no measured gain; the scout costs ~3.5k in the child and 0 in the parent. |
+| Writers | Code is written only in the main session. Workers (`scout --rw`) and auto mode are retired (2026-11-14). | A fresh session started from a card is a worker you can watch and steer; auto mode and its git guard reached every repo on the machine. |
+| Unit of work | The `/plan` card: outcome, done-when, interface, mechanism, files, out. One card per slice session. | An outcome without mechanism and interface invites the cheapest route to green checks. |
+| Slice check | `bin/slice-check` reruns the done-when line and fails on files outside the card, lost test cases or assertions, and new skip/only markers. `/plan` "go" runs it. | A rule stated in prose failed 30% of the time; a check in code went 0 of 8. |
+| Second review | `scout --review <base>` reviews the working tree in a fresh read-only context, on request. | Fresh-context read-only review is the subagent pattern that holds up. |
+| Testing | Red-first for bugs only; new behaviour is tested through its settled interface; expected values never come from the code under test. `/review` breaks each new test's code once. | Test-first on new code fixes the interface before it is designed; agents assert whatever the code returns. |
+| Sessions | `compact-cap` (210k) tells you to `/handoff` and start fresh; it never compacts. Native compaction is the backstop; `prime-reminder` points back to the rules after one. | Session length is the cost lever, and compaction is where rules got lost. |
+| Prompt | `AGENTS.md`, `AGENTS.work.md` and `CLAUDE.md` are generated from `agents-md/` by `build-agents.sh`; edit fragments only. Judgment and scope first, the ladder last. | Primacy and recency get used; the middle decays. Shrinking AGENTS.md raised output tokens 29%, so cuts are for salience, never tokens. |
+| Modes | `/plan`, `/review`, `/memory`, `/challenge`, `/grill`, `/diagnose`, `/handoff`: prompt templates, no tool gating. | Steers on demand cost nothing when unused. |
+| Questions | `pi-ask-user`, plus `ask-user-args.ts` to repair OpenAI-shaped batches (pi-ask-user#88). | One structured question beats a wrong implementation loop. |
+| Context | `pi-rtk-optimizer` compacts noisy tool output; web tools are deferred until `web_enable`; `docs.ts` replaces context7's tools. | Measured cold start dropped 16%; tool output, not the prompt, fills sessions. |
+| Skills | Five shared skills from `~/.agents/skills`; package skills off. | pi has no skill-listing budget, so every description is resident every turn. |
+| Jobs and stalls | `background.ts` caps bash and watches jobs; `stream-watchdog.ts` aborts a silent model stream and resumes once. | Every long-running path needs a limit or a health signal. |
+| Placement | Each job lives in the tool that owns it (git hook, CLI, script); an extension needs a harness-only reason. | Same behaviour under pi, Claude Code and a bare shell. |
+| Scout git guard | `bin/guard/git` is first on PATH in every scout and refuses state-changing git against the scout's repo. | PATH catches every call of `git` by name; it is a tripwire, not a sandbox. |
+| Worktrees | Branches first; `wt` worktrees only when two trees must be live. `worktree-setup` runs from the post-checkout and post-merge template hooks and wt's post-start. | One copy path for npm and pnpm repos; agents never edit env files. |
+| UI | `pi-minimalist` plus patched copies (`polish-chrome`, `context-bar`, `heads-up`, `background`, `split-diff`, `kitty-links`). | Copies, not packages, because each needed a patch. |
+| Tracking | Files live here, symlinked into `~/.pi/agent/` by `install.sh`; pi itself is the managed install. | Version-controlled; the brew formula lags releases. |
 
